1
00:00:00,120 --> 00:00:00,940
Two fathers,

2
00:00:01,680 --> 00:00:02,340
same street,

3
00:00:02,980 --> 00:00:03,800
same salary,

4
00:00:04,540 --> 00:00:06,020
same love for their sons.

5
00:00:07,060 --> 00:00:09,120
One gave his son everything money could

6
00:00:09,160 --> 00:00:09,480
buy.

7
00:00:10,160 --> 00:00:12,520
The other gave him something cheaper and

8
00:00:12,680 --> 00:00:13,740
much harder to give.

9
00:00:14,700 --> 00:00:17,120
Twenty years later, only one of them gets

10
00:00:17,160 --> 00:00:18,400
a phone call on Sunday.

11
00:00:19,820 --> 00:00:21,740
Both boys were born the same winter.

12
00:00:22,420 --> 00:00:24,160
Both fathers held them in the same

13
00:00:24,200 --> 00:00:26,940
hospital corridor and made the same quiet

14
00:00:27,040 --> 00:00:29,720
promise, "You'll have a better life than I

15
00:00:29,760 --> 00:00:29,960
did."

16
00:00:31,260 --> 00:00:33,160
Adam decided the best way to keep that

17
00:00:33,200 --> 00:00:34,320
promise was to work.

18
00:00:34,940 --> 00:00:37,060
Extra shifts, weekend contracts.

19
00:00:37,700 --> 00:00:40,040
More money meant more chances for his son,

20
00:00:40,720 --> 00:00:43,180
not because he loved less, because this

21
00:00:43,240 --> 00:00:45,560
was the only kind of love he had ever been

22
00:00:45,660 --> 00:00:45,980
shown.

23
00:00:47,220 --> 00:00:49,800
Sam worked too, but at six o'clock, he

24
00:00:49,860 --> 00:00:50,760
closed the laptop.

25
00:00:51,500 --> 00:00:53,740
Every evening, one hour belonged to his

26
00:00:53,800 --> 00:00:54,080
son.

27
00:00:54,680 --> 00:00:55,880
It cost him a promotion.

28
00:00:56,500 --> 00:00:57,780
He never told anyone.

29
00:00:58,960 --> 00:01:01,280
When Leo turned five, Adam missed the

30
00:01:01,340 --> 00:01:02,920
party, a client meeting,

31
00:01:03,560 --> 00:01:05,660
but he came home with the biggest toy car

32
00:01:05,700 --> 00:01:06,260
in the shop.

33
00:01:06,940 --> 00:01:08,740
Leo played with it for two days.

34
00:01:09,740 --> 00:01:11,480
Max got a small paper kite.

35
00:01:12,120 --> 00:01:14,200
He and his father flew it every Friday

36
00:01:14,260 --> 00:01:14,740
that summer.

37
00:01:15,400 --> 00:01:16,540
It tore in August.

38
00:01:17,240 --> 00:01:19,980
Max is 30 now. He still remembers that

39
00:01:20,020 --> 00:01:20,320
kite.

40
00:01:21,500 --> 00:01:22,240
The school play.

41
00:01:23,220 --> 00:01:25,560
Adam's seat stayed empty, but he'd paid

42
00:01:25,600 --> 00:01:28,280
for the best costume in the class. "Next

43
00:01:28,340 --> 00:01:29,480
time," he promised.

44
00:01:30,140 --> 00:01:31,600
There was always a next time.

45
00:01:32,580 --> 00:01:35,120
Sam sat in the front row, filming badly

46
00:01:35,140 --> 00:01:36,380
and clapping too loud.

47
00:01:37,080 --> 00:01:39,380
Max pretended to be embarrassed. He

48
00:01:39,440 --> 00:01:39,780
wasn't.

49
00:01:40,760 --> 00:01:42,600
Ask grown-up children what they remember

50
00:01:42,780 --> 00:01:43,960
most about their parents.

51
00:01:44,560 --> 00:01:45,740
It's rarely the gifts.

52
00:01:46,360 --> 00:01:47,140
It's the time.

53
00:01:48,180 --> 00:01:51,320
At 15, Leo had the newest phone, the best

54
00:01:51,420 --> 00:01:53,700
shoes, and a closed bedroom door.

55
00:01:54,480 --> 00:01:55,520
Adam knocked once.

56
00:01:56,520 --> 00:01:57,920
Adam: "Need anything?"

57
00:01:58,680 --> 00:01:59,720
Leo: "No."

58
00:02:00,520 --> 00:02:01,440
Conversation over.

59
00:02:02,480 --> 00:02:04,680
Max and his father argued about grades,

60
00:02:04,960 --> 00:02:07,820
curfews, everything. But every Friday,

61
00:02:08,120 --> 00:02:09,860
they still walked to the tea stall at the

62
00:02:09,919 --> 00:02:10,240
corner.

63
00:02:10,979 --> 00:02:13,000
Fights end, walks continue.

64
00:02:14,040 --> 00:02:16,560
One night, both boys had the worst day of

65
00:02:16,600 --> 00:02:17,640
their teenage lives.

66
00:02:18,320 --> 00:02:20,480
Leo locked his door and texted a friend.

67
00:02:21,100 --> 00:02:22,620
Max knocked on his father's.

68
00:02:23,740 --> 00:02:25,620
Children don't go to the parent who gave

69
00:02:25,660 --> 00:02:28,280
them the most. They go to the one who was

70
00:02:28,340 --> 00:02:28,640
there.

71
00:02:29,820 --> 00:02:33,320
Leo grew up successful, polite, busy. He

72
00:02:33,360 --> 00:02:35,480
sends money home every month because

73
00:02:35,540 --> 00:02:37,200
that's what his father taught him love

74
00:02:37,240 --> 00:02:37,780
looks like.

75
00:02:38,540 --> 00:02:41,420
Max grew up busy too, but every Sunday at

76
00:02:41,500 --> 00:02:44,460
eight, his father's phone rings. He always

77
00:02:44,500 --> 00:02:44,960
picks up.

78
00:02:46,140 --> 00:02:48,440
Adam retires with a comfortable house, a

79
00:02:48,480 --> 00:02:50,720
full bank account, and a quiet phone.

80
00:02:51,480 --> 00:02:53,100
He scrolls through old photos.

81
00:02:53,720 --> 00:02:56,320
Leo's birthday party, Leo's school play,

82
00:02:56,840 --> 00:02:59,300
Leo's graduation. In every picture,

83
00:02:59,680 --> 00:03:01,400
someone else is holding the camera.

84
00:03:02,760 --> 00:03:05,640
Sam's knees are weaker now, so Max walks

85
00:03:05,700 --> 00:03:07,960
slower. They still go to the same tea

86
00:03:08,080 --> 00:03:09,560
stall, same bench,

87
00:03:10,180 --> 00:03:11,900
25 years of Fridays.

88
00:03:13,020 --> 00:03:15,980
That Sunday, Adam picks up the phone, not

89
00:03:16,020 --> 00:03:16,680
to send money.

90
00:03:17,680 --> 00:03:18,920
Adam: "Leo,

91
00:03:19,760 --> 00:03:21,160
do you have time this weekend?

92
00:03:21,800 --> 00:03:23,040
Not for anything, just

93
00:03:23,660 --> 00:03:23,980
time."

94
00:03:25,100 --> 00:03:27,360
Leo: "I'll come on Friday, Dad."

95
00:03:28,620 --> 00:03:30,760
Your children won't remember most of what

96
00:03:30,800 --> 00:03:32,880
you bought them. They'll remember who

97
00:03:32,920 --> 00:03:35,420
showed up, and it's never too late to

98
00:03:35,480 --> 00:03:36,360
start showing up.
